From 620aa69247696e75ab25ea27c9c5f74012209ccf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fangjian Ju <roger@FangjiandeMacBook-Pro.local>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 13:34:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] [rh2-integration] skip optimizer step on exactly-zero
 global gradient

After all microbatch forward/backward passes and gradient reductions,
train_one_step now scans the accumulated main_grad/grad for local
any_nonzero/all_finite facts and MAX-reduces them across all trainer
ranks so every rank reaches the same verdict. An exactly-zero global
gradient (no epsilon; NaN/Inf compare non-equal to zero so corruption
can never be masked) yields the new non-fatal SKIPPED_ZERO_SIGNAL
outcome: the batch is consumed and grads are cleared, but
optimizer.step()/scheduler.step() are skipped so parameters,
exp_avg/exp_avg_sq and the LR schedule stay untouched (AdamW weight
decay would otherwise mutate weights with no training signal).
Non-zero finite gradients and the pre-existing found-inf /
check_for_nan error paths are unchanged; multi-LoRA and
debug_disable_optimizer are excluded from the scan.

train() now returns a TrainRolloutReport aggregated over all optimizer
steps of the call (applied_optimizer_steps / skipped_zero_signal_steps
/ weights_dirty): with several steps per call, only the last outcome
cannot tell the driver whether any real update happened. The report is
a str subclass carrying the rollout-level outcome as its value, so
every existing `== TrainStepOutcome.X` comparison (actor.py, FT group)
keeps working without modification and instances survive Ray pickling.

A configurable circuit breaker (args.max_consecutive_zero_signal_steps,
injectable via --custom-config-path until a CLI flag exists; <=0
disables) stops the job with diagnostics after N consecutive
zero-signal steps, counted per optimizer instance.

Scheduling, staleness and queue semantics are untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
